#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280636 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37e00ff7-a91e-5b56-9f3a-24e7318cf004" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Convergence Sequences Containing Power and Logarithmic Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a2b5978-ba0f-592b-822c-5e0e2c1a9446" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280638} {:answer/id #uuid "1f1a164d-bcf6-5d0b-b580-549e63d2d8a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280639} {:answer/id #uuid "3ed88fde-ad9c-54ba-89d4-38223e9974e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280640} {:answer/id #uuid "a932a83a-9095-5f0e-8e23-49741ae40df6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280641} {:answer/id #uuid "e79fae98-a615-5226-8fce-b5e94bc346e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The sequence diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280642}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ed88fde-ad9c-54ba-89d4-38223e9974e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280640} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7549977-c45d-5f32-b72c-f3f9bdbe8bd6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280637}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "077153d9-f94d-53b5-9967-cba313acfa27" :question/math-academy-id "q-123707" :question/problem "Does the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{log}_{3}⁡(100n)}{n\\sqrt{n}}$ for $n\\ge 1$ converge or diverge? If the sequence converges, what is its limit?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nWe calculate the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches infinity:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{log}_{3}⁡(100n)}{n\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{log}_{3}⁡(100n)}{n\\sqrt{n}}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sequence converges,  and its limit is $0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280643 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "51d50237-f6cf-5c2d-b8d1-335378205a28" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Convergence of Sequences Containing Exponential Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04fc6693-9aa3-5041-96df-f89d92ba5ae4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280645} {:answer/id #uuid "410c7c1d-3d7a-5045-a258-e543f110a569" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280646} {:answer/id #uuid "6f047844-de48-5145-a031-3fc7f046f591" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The sequence diverges." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280647} {:answer/id #uuid "f6b04115-b3fc-5205-8e9a-02656bf30804" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280648} {:answer/id #uuid "3f1c2eaa-6b51-5f14-8ed0-764d61f58df9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280649}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6f047844-de48-5145-a031-3fc7f046f591" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The sequence diverges." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280647} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c124ee9-94cf-5539-b964-4c65589210ec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280644}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "07f019fc-52c4-5337-bbad-e5807016d73f" :question/math-academy-id "q-123714" :question/problem "What is the limit of the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{e}^{n/10}}{{log}_{5}⁡n}-1$ for $n\\ge 1?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nTo find the limit, we calculate\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{{e}^{n/10}}{{log}_{5}⁡n}-1)=-1+{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{n/10}}{{log}_{5}⁡n}.\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the numerator is growing much faster than the denominator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{n/10}}{{log}_{5}⁡n}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{{e}^{n/10}}{{log}_{5}⁡n}-1)=\\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sequence diverges."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101363 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37e00ff7-a91e-5b56-9f3a-24e7318cf004" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Convergence Sequences Containing Power and Logarithmic Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d543c0b8-45ec-504a-820d-e29a4c8ecec7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099775} {:answer/id #uuid "db16aa56-4b6e-5fe2-88c8-cb85d088e7c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099776} {:answer/id #uuid "ecdb8b81-42ba-513c-a7ac-673a092cd2ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099777} {:answer/id #uuid "fc73f838-7572-5684-a623-0f20baee27cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099778} {:answer/id #uuid "7af81254-d72b-5e4f-a689-5964012cdc31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The sequence diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099779}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d543c0b8-45ec-504a-820d-e29a4c8ecec7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099775} :answer-field/id #uuid "20331583-af52-5bba-9d4e-9c64033ae48a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099774}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6658a5d5-f7b4-5df2-a9a7-e76a081c1513" :question/math-academy-id "q-49661" :question/problem "Does the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡n}{{n}^{2}}$ for $n\\ge 1$ converge or diverge? If the sequence converges, what is its limit?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nWe calculate the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches infinity:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡n}{{n}^{2}}\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡n}{{n}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sequence converges,  and its limit is $0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280650 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "51d50237-f6cf-5c2d-b8d1-335378205a28" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Convergence of Sequences Containing Exponential Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c45892a5-fa4e-59b5-977d-61eb272cef33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${b}_{n}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280652} {:answer/id #uuid "234e8662-817b-5e07-a98b-ffb415f965a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${a}_{n}$ and ${b}_{n}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280653} {:answer/id #uuid "25faa093-7811-5eec-bd3d-f873e1105f6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${c}_{n}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280654} {:answer/id #uuid "6ad4103d-49aa-55ff-a691-1c7ced9bf9fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${b}_{n}$ and ${c}_{n}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280655} {:answer/id #uuid "abf315db-c1da-52cd-a7bd-0fe0bfe090de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${a}_{n}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280656}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "abf315db-c1da-52cd-a7bd-0fe0bfe090de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${a}_{n}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280656} :answer-field/id #uuid "4046cbdf-257a-5bdd-adbd-f63ad89feeef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280651}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "57946c6b-cbfd-57f6-9f11-08ecdcc2f113" :question/math-academy-id "q-49734" :question/problem "Which of the following sequences, defined for $n>1,$ diverge?\n\n- ${a}_{n}=\\frac{n\\sqrt{n}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n)}$\n- ${b}_{n}=\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡(2n)}{{e}^{n}}$\n- ${c}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{5}}{{e}^{n}}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nTo find the limit of each sequence, we must evaluate them for $n\\to \\infty .$\n\nFor ${a}_{n}=\\frac{n\\sqrt{n}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n)},$ both the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the numerator is growing much faster than the denominator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nFor ${b}_{n}=\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡(2n)}{{e}^{n}},$ both the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{b}_{n}=0.\n$$\n\nFor ${c}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{5}}{{e}^{n}},$ both the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{c}_{n}=0.\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"${a}_{n}$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280657 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37e00ff7-a91e-5b56-9f3a-24e7318cf004" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Convergence Sequences Containing Power and Logarithmic Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "524251b7-b5ad-5e06-9944-783aff95dcf9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280659} {:answer/id #uuid "305d791e-48f8-5c83-b0a6-452991cfdb0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280660} {:answer/id #uuid "b0c0d996-d20d-529a-a99a-52b143696771" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280661} {:answer/id #uuid "a29159f0-3300-5b4c-8015-adf0d50b0270" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280662} {:answer/id #uuid "057b99fc-79a5-5517-8523-26c57ecf8038" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The sequence diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280663}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "057b99fc-79a5-5517-8523-26c57ecf8038" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The sequence diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280663} :answer-field/id #uuid "e84b5bf5-33fc-5944-98f9-2d7eef6825b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280658}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a321a1e4-d59b-5239-a7ef-bd1350c42b74" :question/math-academy-id "q-75948" :question/problem "What is the limit of the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{4{n}^{3}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2n)}+10$ for $n\\ge 1?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nWe calculate the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches infinity:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{4{n}^{3}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2n)}+10)=10+{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{4{n}^{3}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2n)}\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the numerator is growing much faster than the denominator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{4{n}^{3}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2n)}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{4{n}^{3}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2n)}+10)=\\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sequence diverges."}]}
